<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Dila'iyya</h1><p class="st-meta">1566 CE – 1668 CE (approx.) · capital Dila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sufi lodge in the Middle Atlas that grew into a Berber state, ruling much of northern Morocco during the collapse of the Saadis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1566 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-muhammad">Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad</a> al-Majati founded a zawiya at Dila in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-atlas">Middle Atlas</a>, among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja Berber</a> tribes. It became a renowned centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teaching and scholarship, attracting students and gifts, and grew wealthy and influential as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a> weakened after 1603.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under Muhammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, from 1636, the zawiya became a political power. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> forces defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marabout-state-of-al-ayyashi">Marabout state of al-Ayyashi</a> in 1641, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> and controlled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-sale">Republic of Salé</a>. The Dila'iyya combined religious prestige, tribal alliances and trade, and their lodge was famous for its library and teachers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The rising <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Moulay al-Rashid</a> defeated the Dila'iyya in 1668 and destroyed the zawiya, dispersing its family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. Moroccan unity was restored under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouites</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 1566 CE – 1668 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.87%;width:2.03%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1566 CE – 1668 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Bakr al-Dila'i, Muhammad al-Hajj</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufi)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zawiya of Dila, Dilaite state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1617&amp;focus=dilaiyya" aria-label="Dila'iyya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4495 1025 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dila'iyya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 1200h0M4858 1205h0M4826 1200h0M4826 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4815 1281h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4815 1281h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Morocco. <a href="/atlas?year=1617&amp;focus=dilaiyya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
